00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:13.830
As your plane descends, beginning its landing into Nur-Sultan, you see the beautiful green

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.880
steppe which covers Kazakhstan.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:20.640
Or, in winter, sprawling rows of snow as far
as the eye can see, making Nur-Sultan the

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.520
second-coldest capital in the world.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.020
Before you even step foot on its soil, the
view from your seat is a quite literal window

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.199
into Kazakhstan as a whole.

00:00:30.199 --> 00:00:35.389
The shining blue dome of the airport is a
preview of the weird, futuristic architecture

00:00:35.389 --> 00:00:37.030
to come.

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:42.559
On both sides is the airport’s name - written
in Cyrillic on one side and Latin on the other,

00:00:42.559 --> 00:00:45.769
reflecting its ethnic and linguistic diversity.

00:00:45.769 --> 00:00:50.050
The letters on the left, however, will soon
be made obsolete.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:59.879
On October 26th, 2017, Nazarbayev issued Presidential
Decree 569, changing the entire Kazakh alphabet

00:00:59.879 --> 00:01:02.659
from Cyrillic to one based on Latin.

00:01:02.659 --> 00:01:05.789
This letter, for example, will become this
one.

00:01:05.789 --> 00:01:09.090
This, will become this, and so on.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:16.179
By 2025, every letter in this 18-million person
country - every sign, textbook, menu, street

00:01:16.179 --> 00:01:20.969
sign, website, and logo, will be rewritten,
and teachers, retrained.

00:01:20.969 --> 00:01:26.369
The government has budgeted $600 million for
this transition, about 90% of which will be

00:01:26.369 --> 00:01:31.119
spent teaching the public how to read and
write this newly invented alphabet.

00:01:31.119 --> 00:01:36.200
Officially the reason for this extraordinary
project is simple: the 42-symbols of the Cyrillic

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.770
alphabet make typing difficult.

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:43.609
Latin simplifies the language, making it easier
to learn, write, and type.

00:01:43.609 --> 00:01:46.749
The truth is far more complicated.

00:01:46.749 --> 00:01:47.460
And, political.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:54.980
Split between Europe and Asia, Kazakhstan
is a lot of things…

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:57.979
The 9th largest country measured by area,

00:01:57.979 --> 00:02:00.320
the largest landlocked country,

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:03.999
and the 3rd most Russian speaking country
in the world.

00:02:03.999 --> 00:02:09.190
But its defining feature might be its lack
of a single, unifying feature.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:15.709
Kazakhstan is a nation of many - many languages,
cultures, cuisines, even holidays.

00:02:15.709 --> 00:02:21.650
Officially, it celebrates three different
New Years: January 1st, then the 14th - a

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:26.970
leftover from the Julian calendar of its Soviet
days, and again in March.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:32.230
Once entirely nomadic, Kazakhstan’s land
was invaded by the Mongol Empire and colonized

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:36.590
by the Russian Empire, before establishing
brief independence.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:40.430
In 1920 it became a Soviet autonomous republic.

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:45.939
Russia encouraged its transformation from
nomadic groups into settlements, but its collectivization

00:02:45.939 --> 00:02:51.569
efforts killed over a million and a half people,
one-fifth of its population at the time, mostly

00:02:51.569 --> 00:02:54.330
due to starvation and disease.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:03:00.099
During this time, many Kazakhs fled to neighboring
Uzbekistan, Turkmenistan, and China.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:05.160
In 1936, Kazakhstan became a full, constituent
republic of the Soviet Union.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:11.060
Its northeastern town now called Semey was
used by Moscow as the site of its first nuclear

00:03:11.069 --> 00:03:16.750
bomb test, followed by hundreds more, until
the ecological and biological consequences

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:18.110
were impossible to ignore.

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:19.730
To this day, the country pays dearly for its
past.

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:24.510
In 2013, residents of a small village called
Kalachi began falling asleep for days or even

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:26.319
weeks at a time.

00:03:26.319 --> 00:03:31.489
Before killing 152 people, doctors suspected
counterfeit vodka was the culprit.

00:03:31.489 --> 00:03:38.090
A global investigation eventually revealed
the cause: a nearby abandoned Soviet-era Uranium

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:39.090
mine.

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:44.810
On the 16th of December, 1991, Kazakhstan
declared independence, 10 days before the

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:47.640
Soviet Union, itself, dissolved.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:52.700
Like its neighbors, the new state found its
factories underperforming, economy failing,

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.720
and the mouths of its people desperate to
be fed.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:04:02.500
But Kazakhstan was unique in one major respect:
it was, by far, the most ethnically diverse.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:12.849
In 1989, Armenia was 93% Armenian, Lithuania,
79% Lithuanian, and Georgia, 70% Georgian.

00:04:12.849 --> 00:04:17.310
Kazakhstan, on the other hand, just 39% Kazakh.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:23.870
It is and long has been a giant blend of people
- thanks, in large part, to Soviet migration.

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:30.240
Between 1907 and 12, newly built railways
brought 2.4 million people from central Russia

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.410
to North Kazakhstan.

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:39.610
Another 1.7 million migrants arrived between
54 and 62 in the Soviet Union’s Virgin Lands

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:43.060
campaign to boost agricultural output.

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:47.850
Migration within the Soviet Union wasn’t
especially political at the time,

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:52.390
But after its fall, these mere details of
history became a serious challenge to the

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:57.810
sovereignty of Kazakhstan, who, lacking a
unified ethnic group, had to fight extra hard

00:04:57.810 --> 00:04:59.850
to be seen as a viable state.

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:05.920
Kazakhs, at 40% of the population, were themselves
a minority, despite being the name of the

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:06.920
country.

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:12.560
Slavs, with 44%, often felt displaced by the
new political climate and threatened to form

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:14.060
their own state.

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:18.350
In all, 2.6 million people left Kazakhstan
in the 90s.

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:21.060
Mostly, Russians returning to Russia.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:25.670
In modern times, Putin has been known to make
subtle threats which question the legitimacy

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:31.190
of its southern neighbor, suggesting it may
lack the unity necessary for statehood.

00:05:31.190 --> 00:05:35.550
At the same time, Russia is its most important
ally.

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:40.410
Kazakhstan has the 12th largest proven oil
reserves in the world, on par with China or

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:41.550
the U.S.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:46.960
This alone is the single biggest factor responsible
for its economic prosperity when compared

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:51.070
to other Central Asian nations like Kyrgyzstan
and Uzbekistan.

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:55.960
Unfortunately, as you may remember, it has
no direct access to the sea.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:06:00.590
This forces it to transport much of its oil
through Russia, to ports in the Black Sea,

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:01.590
for example.

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:05.480
This was and continues to be the central challenge
of Kazakhstan.

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:11.380
At a time when Russia is eager to expand its
borders, Kazakhstan must perform the delicate

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:17.280
balancing act of actively asserting its sovereignty,
while, at the same time, not angering its

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:19.620
most important ally.

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:24.720
Because anything too aggressive might prompt
a response from Russia, this usually manifests

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:29.210
as soft, cultural and symbolic changes designed
to prove its identity.

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:31.800
It’s language, for example.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:37.000
In the late 30s, the Soviet Union feared its
minority ethnic groups might develop a shared,

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:38.830
non-Soviet identity.

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:43.550
To prevent this, it created almost 20 different
varieties of the Cyrillic alphabet for each

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:46.500
of their languages, including Kazakh.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:49.840
During this time, Russian spread rapidly to
Kazakhstan.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:55.030
So, while Kazakh has long been its “state
language”, Russian is still more widely

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:56.090
spoken.

00:06:56.090 --> 00:07:01.150
Even more confusing - because Kazakh was written
in Arabic script before Cyrillic, those who

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:06.460
fled to China or elsewhere during Soviet times
will return without knowing how to read or

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:08.490
write their own language.

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:14.590
Since the collapse of the Soviet Union, Moldova,
Azerbaijan, Uzbekistan, and Turkmenistan have

00:07:14.590 --> 00:07:16.410
changed their alphabets.

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:21.680
But, because of its diversity, Kazakhstan
has been more careful to maintain stability,

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:23.770
only now following their lead.

00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:30.280
The original, 2017 plan was to use a 32 letter
Latin-based alphabet, but it was revised last

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:35.410
year after public backlash over its nine letters
with apostrophes, which made the language

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:37.940
unnecessarily complicated.

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:43.470
Although not an official goal, the government
also manages its ethnic blend through immigration.

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:48.870
A special returnee program offers incentives
like one-time relocation payments, subsidized

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:53.750
housing, and expedited citizenship for overseas
Kazakhs to return home.

00:07:53.750 --> 00:08:01.430
Almaty, its most populous city, has gone from
22% Kazakh at independence to 60% in 2018.

00:08:01.430 --> 00:08:04.940
17 to 78% in Nur-Sultan.

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:09.660
Immigrants are specifically encouraged to
settle in its northern towns bordering Russia,

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:15.940
some of which are 60 or 80% Russian, under
the veneer of offsetting labor shortages.

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:20.610
These Slavic areas are carefully monitored
for separatists by the government, which doesn’t

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:22.710
hesitate to make arrests.

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:27.890
Towering above one town is a not-so-subtle
reminder of its authority: “Kazakhstan”

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:30.200
written in giant white letters.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:35.269
One family dispute between ethnic groups in
2015 was quickly met with riot-gear wearing

00:08:35.269 --> 00:08:40.870
police, who shut down all internet within
a 120-kilometer radius to prevent the spread

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:42.479
of conflict.

00:08:42.479 --> 00:08:47.920
Russian Nationalists, in turn, use incidents
like these to argue the mistreatment of Slavs,

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:50.560
and the need for intervention on their behalf.

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:56.740
Finally, if there was still any doubt of Kazakhstan’s
desire to assert control in its Slavic-North,

00:08:56.740 --> 00:09:02.649
in 1997, it made the drastic decision to move
its capital from Almaty to a new planned city

00:09:02.649 --> 00:09:07.279
called Astana, later renamed Nur-Sultan in
2019.

00:09:07.279 --> 00:09:13.629
Again, the change was made on the pretense
that Almaty was too small and at risk of earthquakes.

00:09:13.629 --> 00:09:18.580
Like other declarations made by cultish dictators,
it was met with laughter from the foreign

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:19.580
press.

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:24.699
In reality, it was a calculated step to exert
political power in the more Russian North.

00:09:24.699 --> 00:09:30.839
It’s peculiar, futuristic architecture very
intentionally paints a picture of a modern,

00:09:30.839 --> 00:09:34.959
prosperous city, not another ex-Soviet “something-stan”.

00:09:34.959 --> 00:09:37.920
All the while, it continues pleasing Russia.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:43.910
Its city Baikonur, for example, is leased
to Russia until 2050 for use as the first

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:46.660
and largest running spaceport.

00:09:46.660 --> 00:09:53.639
Because of Soviet-era construction, some Kazakhstani
rail routes even pass through Russian territory.

00:09:53.639 --> 00:09:57.600
For the most part, Russia is satisfied with
this arrangement.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:02.100
Kazakhstan has ensured regional stability
and acted as a buffer, protecting Moscow from

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:04.050
less amicable states.

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:08.970
But the implied warning beneath Putin’s
occasional subtle threats is that its borders

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:14.480
could easily be… embraced should the country
ever stop being useful.

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:18.850
In recent years, China has emerged as a powerful
new economic partner.

00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:24.430
Today, a quarter of its oil is controlled
by Chinese corporations, and the border town

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:29.540
of Khorgos has been turned into a major trade
hub where trains from Western Europe connect

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:35.620
with Eastern China, saving weeks of travel
by boat or millions of dollars, by air.

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:40.560
Although its profitability is in doubt, it
has become the poster child for China’s

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:44.360
$1 Trillion Dollar Belt and Road investment
crusade.

00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:50.279
The Kazakhstan government argues China is
the key to decreasing its dependence on oil.

00:10:50.279 --> 00:10:52.960
And, so far, it’s worked.

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:57.540
Kazakstan is a model example of how to satisfy
a diverse mix of people.

00:10:57.540 --> 00:11:03.759
It’s preserved Russia as its security ally,
appeased its own ethnic Russians, and fostered

00:11:03.759 --> 00:11:09.019
a new relationship with China on its eastern
border, all while outperforming the economies

00:11:09.019 --> 00:11:10.889
of its Central Asian neighbors.

00:11:10.889 --> 00:11:16.139
It’s managed all this by creating its own
brutal flavor of authoritarianism.

00:11:16.139 --> 00:11:22.399
As the nation’s only president from 1991
until this year, the personality cult of Nursultan

00:11:22.399 --> 00:11:25.589
Nazarbayev rivaled that of Kim Jong-Un.

00:11:25.589 --> 00:11:31.050
He once humbly declared, “I have categorically
put a stop to all praise directed at me and

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:33.959
proposals to especially glorify me personally…

00:11:33.959 --> 00:11:39.339
I have always tried to be above all kinds
of praise and eulogies and I will continue

00:11:39.339 --> 00:11:41.379
to uphold this position.”

00:11:41.379 --> 00:11:46.499
The capital city, its airport, and university
were then named in his honor.

00:11:46.499 --> 00:11:54.160
After winning 97.7% of the vote in 2015 elections,
Nazarbayev apologized, suggesting he could

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:57.700
have intervened to lower his margin, but that
would’ve looked undemocratic.

00:11:57.700 --> 00:11:59.800
He also once said public servants ought to
retire after 25 years, before continuing to

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:00.800
serve for 29.

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:04.899
52% of the population was born during his
rule, leading to extreme political stability,

00:12:04.899 --> 00:12:10.509
and a country which rarely receives international
attention, despite being every bit as dictatorial

00:12:10.509 --> 00:12:12.310
as Russia or China.

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:17.290
This, however, is the inherent vulnerability
of authoritarianism.

00:12:17.290 --> 00:12:23.259
Its true fragility is never quite known - discontent
is masked by fear of punishment for speaking

00:12:23.259 --> 00:12:28.850
out, until, all at once, it’s let loose,
and the previously restrained anger suddenly

00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:30.309
boils over.

00:12:30.309 --> 00:12:35.640
A perfectly stable-appearing authoritarian
state can rapidly descend into chaos, but

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:40.879
it’s impossible to know when or even if
such a revolution is coming.

00:12:40.879 --> 00:12:44.139
For Kazakhstan, there are already warning
signs:

00:12:44.139 --> 00:12:49.579
China’s detention of Uyghurs and Kazakhs
within its borders has led to a rapid growth

00:12:49.579 --> 00:12:55.290
in anti-China sentiment in Kazakhstan, where
China’s involvement is seen as a liability,

00:12:55.290 --> 00:12:56.350
not an opportunity.

00:12:56.350 --> 00:13:01.410
So, when the government announced a plan to
auction farmland to Chinese investors, many

00:13:01.410 --> 00:13:03.079
citizens protested.

00:13:03.079 --> 00:13:09.079
Russia’s annexation of Crimea has also made
the country wary, as the next largest Russian

00:13:09.079 --> 00:13:12.160
and energy-rich country on its border.

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:18.209
Domestically, some ethnic Slavs in Kazakhstan
increasingly feel like second-class citizens,

00:13:18.209 --> 00:13:22.720
forced to abandon their own language and alienated
from their own government.

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:28.329
Since its founding, Kazakhstan has been a
nation defined by its central geographic position

00:13:28.329 --> 00:13:35.579
between two continents, two globally-powerful
giants, and hundreds of cultural identities.

00:13:35.579 --> 00:13:43.629
But it remains to be seen whether this fusion
will be its biggest asset, or, it’s undoing.

